field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465212}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ec726d7a-9c79-5a1b-91e2-01dc3e4c2650" :question/math-academy-id "q-158102" :question/problem "![](images/42/42b23af4df7892df46461b01fd549ca56723dde0c08d3e88b71ea119bf45ba23.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a plane that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents the origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,,\\,$ which is considered a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}]}
